# Line endings are part of the build contract here, not a formatting
# preference. Two builds from the same pins must produce byte-identical
# output, and a patch that applies on Linux and fails on Windows is the same
# defect as a patch that does not apply at all.
#
# Without this file, Git for Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true by
# default: every file in this repository arrives with CRLF, while the Chromium
# sources gclient fetches keep LF. `git apply` then sees patch context lines
# ending in \r against source lines that do not, and the series fails to apply
# before a single translation unit is compiled. That is exactly how the first
# Windows run of the compile gate failed.

# Everything this repository stores is LF, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Patch files are byte-exact and must never be translated at all, not even to
# LF. Some legitimately carry literal CRLF because the Chromium file they edit
# does -- 0042-audio-portable-neon-profile.patch is one -- and normalising
# those bytes would corrupt the patch rather than tidy it.
*.patch -text
patches/series -text

# Captures and golden artifacts are compared by hash. Treat them as opaque.
*.json -text
*.jsonl -text
*.lock -text

# Binary assets, listed so nothing infers a text type for them.
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.tar.xz binary
